latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Input Analysis:\n   a. Divide the 4x14 input grid into seven 4x2 sections.\n   b. For each section:\n      - Calculate the density of colored squares (2 and 3).\n      - Identify any linear or diagonal patterns within the section.\n      - Determine the distribution of colors (top-heavy, bottom-heavy, etc.).\n   c. Calculate the overall density of the entire input grid for reference.\n\n2. Initial Output Creation:\n   a. Create a 4x7 output grid, initially all black (0).\n\n3. Column-wise Processing:\n   For each column in the output grid:\n   a. If the corresponding input section has high density (>50% colored):\n      - Fill the entire column with gray (5).\n   b. If medium density (25-50% colored):\n      - Fill 2-3 squares with gray based on the distribution in the input section.\n   c. If low density (<25% colored):\n      - Fill 0-1 squares with gray based on any significant patterns detected.\n\n4. Pattern Continuation:\n   a. Analyze adjacent columns for potential continuations of patterns.\n   b. Extend lines or diagonals if they align with patterns in adjacent columns.\n\n5. Global Pattern Analysis:\n   a. Identify potential lines or shapes that span multiple columns in the input.\n   b. Adjust the output grid to reflect these global patterns:\n      - Connect nearby gray squares to form more continuous shapes.\n      - Add gray squares to complete apparent lines or diagonals.\n\n6. Smoothing and Refinement:\n   a. Connect isolated gray squares if they have gray neighbors in adjacent columns.\n   b. Remove single isolated gray squares that don't contribute to larger patterns.\n   c. Ensure that the density of gray squares in the output is proportional to the overall density of colored squares in the input.\n\n7. Balance Check:\n   a. Calculate the ratio of gray to black squares in the output.\n   b. Compare this to the ratio of colored to black squares in the input.\n   c. Adjust the output if necessary to better match the input's balance:\n      - Add gray squares in sparse areas if the output is too empty.\n      - Remove gray squares from dense areas if the output is too filled.\n\n8. Final Continuity Pass:\n   a. Scan the output grid for any remaining discontinuities or isolated squares.\n   b. Make final adjustments to ensure coherent shapes and patterns:\n      - Connect nearby gray areas if they seem to be part of the same pattern.\n      - Smooth edges of gray regions to create more natural-looking shapes.\n\n9. Validation:\n   a. Compare the final output to the input, ensuring that:\n      - Major patterns and structures are represented.\n      - The balance of filled to empty space is maintained.\n      - The abstraction captures the essence of the input without over-complicating.\n\nThis plan aims to capture both local and global patterns, maintain appropriate density, and create a coherent abstraction of the input grid. It should be flexible enough to handle various input patterns while consistently producing meaningful output that reflects the essential characteristics of the input."
